$DIR/main.p:34:2: ww is passed to ww.Free(), which may free what it looks at or move it out (clear(v.data), vec.p:288) here, but a view of what it holds (ww.Slice()[0].Slice(), line 33) was passed to save, which may keep it at line 33, where the compiler cannot check its uses
    A call that may free what an owner holds ends every view of that memory
    (language spec section 18.3). The view of ww made by ww.Slice()[0].Slice()
    at line 33 went into memory the compiler does not follow, so it cannot check
    that the view is not used after line 34.
    Go keeps memory alive for as long as any pointer refers to it. Plutonium has
    no garbage collector: an owner releases its memory when it is dropped, so a
    view must not be used after the value it looks at has gone.
    Keep the view in a local variable or a field of one, where the compiler
    follows it, or do not make that call while the view is kept
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3314" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:33:2: cannot pass ww.Slice()[0].Slice() to save, which keeps it after returning
    ww.Slice()[0].Slice() points into ww. ww is a local variable of main
    (declared at main.p:29). save stores s in keep.s (main.p:14), so
    ww.Slice()[0].Slice() would outlive ww.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    If save should keep its own copy of ww, change it to take the value, not a
    pointer, or an owner it can keep: an
    owned[pluto/vec.Vec[pluto/vec.Vec[int]]] from new, a
    shared[pluto/vec.Vec[pluto/vec.Vec[int]]], or a library owner such as a
    vec.Vec.
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3003" for a longer explanation)
